 - The loader knows the x6826b's panel (720 x 1640, LK's framebuffer) and touch controller; another MT6768 phone changes those.
+
+## The board header: one loader, many phones
+
+`mtk/loader/mtkloader.c` is one program for every MediaTek phone. What differs between them is a header of constants,
+`mtk/<soc>/loader/board.h`, which `mtk/loader/build.sh` hands over with `-DBOARD_HEADER="..."`. The build picks it up from
+`devices/$DEVICE/device.conf`'s `soc` line: `DEVICE=xaga` finds `mtk/mt6895/loader/board.h`, and any SoC without one builds
+the MT6768's loader, whose values are the defaults in `mtkloader.c` itself. `BOARD=path/to/board.h` overrides either.
+
+The header names the memory map (where the kernel goes, where the marker, the panic log and the pstore zones are), the
+processors and the GIC's redistributor frames, the console UART, the watchdog, the display's overlay and mutex, and the three
+switches a board without the MT6768's hardware needs:
+
+| Macro | 1 (default) | 0 |
+|---|---|---|
+| `XNU_SYSTEM_IMAGE` | read `xnusys` off the flash here, as `md0` | do not look: the root is the payload's ramdisk |
+| `BOARD_TOUCH` | hand the TD4160 its firmware before XNU starts | skip (a controller this bring-up is not written for) |
+| `XNU_BIG_CLOCK` | raise the A75s' PLL | skip (a board whose PLL this is not) |
+| `BOARD_STORAGE_UFS` | read the flash through `mtk/loader/ufs.h` | read it through `mtk/loader/msdc.h` (eMMC) |
+
+`mtk/mt6895/loader/board.h` is the first one written this way; its comments say, for every value, which dump of the phone it
+came from and what it costs if the value is wrong.
+
+## The MT6895
+
+Not built into any configuration yet: `mtk/mt6895/configs/mt6895.config` leaves every MediaTek and Qualcomm driver out and only
+names the SoC and the four cores, so a kernel built for it is the common one with a screen and no drivers of its own. What is
+there is the loader's board (`mtk/mt6895/loader/board.h`, above) and the facts it was written from.
+
+The phone it was worked out on (Redmi Note 11T Pro, `xaga`, 8 GB) differs from the MT6768 in ways that each need a driver
+rather than a recompile:
+
+| | MT6768 (x6826b) | MT6895 (xaga) |
+|---|---|---|
+| storage | eMMC through MSDC0 at `0x11230000` | **UFS 3.1** at `0x112b0000`, `/dev/block/sdcNN`; no MSDC at all |
+| GPU | Mali-G52 MC2 | Mali-G610 (Valhall) at `0x13000000` |
+| touch | OmniVision TD4160 over SPI2, firmware into its TCM | Novatek (`NVTCapacitiveTouchScreen`), no address in the tree |
+| keys | keypad at `0x10010000`, PMIC directly | keypad at `0x1c00e000`, MT6363 over SPMI at `0x1c804000` |
+| watchdog | `0x10007000` | `0x1c007000` |
+| console UART | `0x11002000` | `0x11001000` |
+| display OVL0 | `0x1400b000` | `0x14002000` |
+| GIC redistributor | one frame of `0x100000` | two frames, `0x200000` from `0x0c040000` |
+| cores | 6× A55 + 2× A75, two clusters | 4× A55 + 3× A78 + 1× A78, three clusters |
+
+The first row has two answers, and both are written:
+
+## The UFS host: reading the flash where there is no eMMC
+
+`mtk/loader/msdc.h` speaks MSDC, which is how every earlier MediaTek phone reads its eMMC. The MT6895 has no MSDC0 — its
+`iomem` has no `0x1123xxxx` window at all — so `mtk/loader/ufs.h` takes over, and `board_read()` in `mtkloader.c` is the one
+place that decides which controller answers (`BOARD_STORAGE_UFS`).
+
+What the loader inherits is the same thing `msdc.h` inherits: LK has already brought the link up, set the gear and the lanes,
+read the device and unit descriptors, and left the controller running with a transfer request list of its own in `UTRLBA`.
+So `ufs.h` resets nothing and touches no PHY — it keeps LK's list address, stops the list (`UTRLRSR` 0), points `UTRLBA`/
+`UTRLBAU` at its own, starts it again, and then issues `READ(10)` in one slot and polls the doorbell. The descriptor layout
+(8-dword UTRD, UPIU at the front of the UCD, response at 512, PRD table at 1024) is the one `drivers/storage/hakios.ufshc.c`
+already implements for the kernel; only the base address and the "no UEFI underneath" assumption differ.
+
+Two consequences worth writing down, because they cost time if forgotten:
+
+- The working area cannot live in `.bss`. The loader is loaded at `0x40080000` and Android's `Image`, which `chain_stub`
+  copies it back over, starts at `_start + CHAIN_OFFSET` = `0x400c0000`; 290 KB of descriptors and buffer in `.bss` would
+  push the loader's own end past that and overwrite the image being chained to. `UFS_AREA_PA` in the board header names an
+  absolute area instead (`0x60100000` on xaga, checked against `iomem`).
+- The MMU is off, so it is all 32-bit device accesses and no cache maintenance is needed for DMA.
+
+`UFS_TEST=1` (build with `-DUFS_TEST=1`) reads the controller's state, issues TEST UNIT READY, reads the GPT header and
+`boot_a`'s `ANDROID!` magic, and times 256 KB — then returns to Android. That is the check to run first on a phone, because
+nothing above it has ever talked to a UFS controller and a mistake there is not visible any other way.
+
+The first boot's shape follows from the first row: with `ufs.h` the loader can read the flash at all. It does not follow that
+the system image comes off it yet — `xnusys` is not a partition the vendor's GPT has; `mtk/tools/gpt-carve.py` is what makes
+one out of the tail of `userdata`, and until that has run on a given phone `gpt_find` prints `no xnusys partition on the ufs`
+and `load_system_image` returns 0. That failure is not fatal — `sys_size` stays 0, the device tree's `memory-map` carries the
+payload's RAM disk as `RAMDisk` (not `RAMDisk1`), and the boot is the plain RAM-disk boot — which is why `XNU_SYSTEM_IMAGE` is
+**1** on xaga: the check costs one partition lookup per boot, and the moment the carve exists the loader picks the image up
+with no rebuild. Until then the root is the payload's RAM disk (`rd=md0`); after it, `md0` is the iOS system image and the
+payload's image becomes `md1`, which is the layout `stage0` expects (it mounts `md1` on `/redwood` and runs our `init` from
+there).
+
+Sizing that carve is not free. `userland/system.apfs` is 2492203008 B (2.32 GiB) and xaga's `userdata` is 3072 MiB, so
+`--gib 2` is too small; `--gib 2.5` is what fits and still leaves half a GiB of userdata. The 4 GiB window the loader hands
+XNU (`XNU_PHYS_BASE` 0x100000000 + `XNU_MEM_SIZE` 0x100000000) holds it: kernel at 0x103004000, ramdisk at 0x104408000, the
+2.32 GiB image at 0x108000000, device tree and boot args after it — 1591 MiB left over.
+
+What it does **not** give is a block device in the kernel: `XNU_ROOT_EMMC` stays 0, because `/dev/ufs0` would need the
+kernel's own driver to inherit the live controller, and `hakios.ufshc.c` was written for the Qualcomm case where UEFI's
+UFSDxe stays resident and services a shared page. Nothing on the MediaTek side provides that yet.
+
+
+## Running it on xaga: `mtk/tools/xaga-cycle.sh`
+
+`xnu-cycle.sh` assumes a phone that can be handed a kernel out of RAM. The xaga cannot: `lk_a` registers no `boot:` command
+(checked against the phone's own `lk_a.img` and against `lk.img` from the stock OS2.0.12 ROM — both have zero occurrences of
+`boot:` as a command string), so every run is a write to `boot_a`. It also has no recovery partition, so a `boot_a` that does
+not boot comes back only through fastboot. `xaga-cycle.sh` exists for those two facts:
+
+```sh
+export HAKIOS_SERIAL=<serial from `adb devices`>
+make xaga_defconfig
+mtk/tools/xaga-cycle.sh            # build, flash boot_a, run XNU once, bring the log back
+XAGA_SKIP_FLASH=1 mtk/tools/xaga-cycle.sh   # run again on the image already in boot_a
+```
+
+- It refuses to run at all unless `backup/boot_a_magisk.img` exists. That backup has to be **this phone's** boot image, not
+  the stock one: the phone is rooted through `boot_a`, so restoring stock loses root, and the `su -c` that reads
+  `/sys/fs/pstore/console-ramoops-0` at the end of every run needs it.
+- It checks `current-slot` is `a` before writing, because it only knows how to write `boot_a`.
+- On any failure it prints `fastboot -s $SERIAL flash boot_a backup/boot_a_magisk.img` on screen.
+- The kernel's arguments are `tools/hakios-config.py args` (the `.config`'s), not a hand-written string: `fbfit`/`fbscale`
+  are what make the kernel's framebuffer driver scale onto this panel, and a run without them boots and draws nothing.
+- `XNU_SECS` (45), `XNU_WATCH` (120) and `XNU_ARGS` override the defaults. They are passed to `build.sh` as `-D` flags,
+  which is the only thing it reads (`mtk/loader/build.sh` takes no environment of its own).
+
+## Getting the iOS system image onto the phone: `mtk/tools/xaga-carve.sh`
+
+A RAM-disk boot is the port's userland. An iOS boot needs the 2.32 GiB system image in its own partition, and the vendor's
+GPT has no such partition: `xnusys` has to be carved out of the tail of `userdata` once per phone. That is a GPT rewrite,
+which is the one operation here a mistake does not come back from, so it is its own script and it stops before writing:
+
+```sh
+HAKIOS_SERIAL=<serial> mtk/tools/xaga-carve.sh --gib 2.5
+```
+
+1. It reads the primary GPT (sectors 0..33) and the backup GPT (the last 34) off `/dev/block/sdc` — UFS LUN 2, which is
+   where the stock scatter puts every partition but the two preloaders — and the disk's sector count via `blockdev --getsz`.
+2. It hands those three to `mtk/tools/gpt-carve.py`, which writes `gpt-primary.bin.new` and `gpt-secondary.bin.new` beside
+   them and touches nothing on the phone. Both headers' CRCs and the entry array's CRC come out valid; the check for a
+   partition in the way is a range test against every other entry.
+3. It prints the two `dd … conv=notrunc` commands and the reboot. Nothing is written until those are run.
+
+`--gib 2.5` is not a preference: `userland/system.apfs` is 2.32 GiB, so 2 GiB cannot hold it, and xaga's `userdata` is
+3072 MiB, so 2.5 GiB leaves half a GiB of user data — an Android that still works. The script checks the image's size against
+`--gib` and refuses a value that is too small.
+
+The script refuses to carve a phone that already has `xnusys`: a second carve would make a second partition and shrink
+`userdata` twice.
+
+Once `xnusys` exists and Android is back (`ls -l /dev/block/by-name/xnusys` answers), the image goes in with
+`mtk/tools/system-flash.sh`, which pushes it to `/data/local/tmp` and `dd`s it in — LK's fastboot cannot take a file this
+large, and piping into `adb exec-in` silently drops most of it.
+
+### What the boot looks like on each side of the carve
+
+| | `md0` | `md1` | PID 1 | what runs |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| no `xnusys` yet | the payload's `root.apfs` | — | our `init` | the port's checks, then `etc/rc` |
+| `xnusys` present | the iOS system image | the payload's `root.apfs` | `stage0` | stage0 mounts `md1` on `/redwood`, runs our `init` from it, which ends with `exec /sbin/launchd.ios` — Apple's own launchd, on the system image |
+
+`etc/rc` is one file for both modes: `src/init.c` rewrites `/sbin/launchd.ios` (and `/sbin/launchd.darwin`, the name the
+port's own tree uses) to `/sbin/launchd` when the root is the flash itself, so the same line hands process 1 to the right
+program either way. The name matters because `/sbin/launchd` **in the system image is stage0**, not Apple's launchd:
+`build.sh` moves the real one to `/sbin/launchd.ios` before copying stage0 into its place. Executing `/sbin/launchd` there
+would run stage0 a second time and mount `/redwood` over itself.
